{
  "id": "u03-l02",
  "unit": "u03-family",
  "title": "Elder & younger",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "**বড়** (*boro*) = big, **ছোট** (*chhoto*) = small. With family they mean **elder** and **younger**: **বড় ভাই** = elder brother, **ছোট বোন** = younger sister. Bengali almost always says *which* sibling.\n\nYou don't call an elder sibling by name — that would feel cheeky. Instead say **ভাইয়া** (*bhaiya*, elder brother) and **আপু** (*apu*, elder sister). Younger siblings you *do* call by name. In Kolkata the words are **দাদা** and **দিদি** — but careful: in Bangladesh **দাদা** means *grandfather*, as you'll see next lesson.\n\nEvery pronoun has a 'my/your' form with **-র**: সে → **তার**, ও → **ওর**, উনি → **ওনার**, তুই → **তোর**. Like সে, they don't show gender: **তার নাম** = 'his name' or 'her name'. Use **ওনার** for someone you'd call আপনি, and **তোর** only with people you'd call তুই.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "boro",
      "bn": "বড়",
      "tr": "boro",
      "en": "big",
      "notes": "With siblings it means 'elder': বড় ভাই 'elder brother'."
    },
    {
      "id": "chhoto",
      "bn": "ছোট",
      "tr": "chhoto",
      "en": "small",
      "notes": "With siblings it means 'younger': ছোট বোন 'younger sister'. Also spelled ছোটো."
    },
    {
      "id": "bhaiya",
      "bn": "ভাইয়া",
      "tr": "bhaiya",
      "en": "elder brother",
      "notes": "What you call your elder brother (instead of his name). Also polite address for a slightly older man. Kolkata: দাদা."
    },
    {
      "id": "apu",
      "bn": "আপু",
      "tr": "apu",
      "en": "elder sister",
      "notes": "What you call your elder sister. Also friendly address for a slightly older woman. Kolkata: দিদি."
    },
    {
      "id": "tar",
      "bn": "তার",
      "tr": "tar",
      "en": "his/her",
      "notes": "Possessive of সে."
    },
    {
